<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Tosa Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1601 CE – 1871 CE · capital Kochi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Yamauchi domain on Shikoku whose lower samurai, including Sakamoto Ryoma, helped engineer the end of the shogunate in 1867.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosokabe-morichika">Chosokabe Morichika</a> lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tosa-province">Tosa</a> for joining the western army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> gave the province to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamauchi-kazutoyo">Yamauchi Kazutoyo</a>, who arrived in 1601 and built <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-cochin">Kochi</a> castle. Former Chosokabe retainers resisted and were suppressed.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tosa-province">Tosa</a> was sharply divided between the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a>, descended from Yamauchi followers, and the lower samurai and rural goshi, many of Chosokabe stock. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nonaka-kenzan">Nonaka Kenzan's</a> 17th-century reforms developed land and forests. In the 1860s the lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamauchi-toyoshige">Yamauchi Toyoshige</a>, known as Yodo, steered between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> and loyalists, while the loyalist party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takechi-hanpeita">Takechi Hanpeita</a> was crushed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tosa-province">Tosa</a> men <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakamoto-ryoma">Sakamoto Ryoma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goto-shojiro">Goto Shojiro</a> proposed the peaceful return of power to the emperor, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> accepted in 1867, and Tosa troops fought for the new government. It became <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-cochin">Kochi</a> Prefecture in 1871, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itagaki-taisuke">Itagaki Taisuke</a> later led the democratic movement from Tosa.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tosa-domain.jpg" alt="Tosa Domain" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: 663highland · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kochi_Castle08s3872.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1601 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kochi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yamauchi Kazutoyo, Yamauchi Toyoshige</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kochi Domain, Tosa han</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=tosa-domain" aria-label="Tosa Domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8090 960 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tosa Domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8410 1216L8530 1145M8410 1216L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8530 1145h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8410 1216h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8410 1216h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=tosa-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
